{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "the social network, directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, is a gripping and meticulously crafted film that delves into the creation of facebook and the legal battles that ensued. released in 2010, the movie is both a biographical drama and a sharp commentary on the digital age, ambition, and the cost of success.\n\nthe story primarily follows mark zuckerberg (played brilliantly by jesse eisenberg), the harvard student whose idea for a social networking site spirals into a global phenomenon. the film opens with zuckerberg’s iconic status update, \"zucked,\" setting the tone for his sharp, often abrasive wit and his relentless pursuit of validation and success. his relationship with his girlfriend, erica (rooney mara), serves as a poignant backdrop, highlighting the loneliness and emotional detachment that comes with his obsession.\n\nthe film’s narrative unfolds through a series of flashbacks, framed by the lawsuits zuckerberg faces from his former friend and co-founder, eduardo saverin (andrew garfield), and the winkelvoss twins (armie hammer), who accuse him of stealing their idea. these legal battles are not just about money or credit but about betrayal, ambition, and the ethical dilemmas of building an empire. the dialogue, written by sorkin, is razor-sharp and witty, filled with rapid-fire exchanges that reveal the characters’ intelligence, arrogance, and vulnerabilities.\n\njesse eisenberg’s portrayal of mark zuckerberg is nothing short of masterful. he captures zuckerberg’s social awkwardness, his genius, and his moral ambiguity with precision. andrew garfield shines as eduardo saverin, whose loyalty and eventual betrayal add emotional weight to the story. armie hammer delivers a dual performance as the winkelvoss twins, portraying their arrogance and entitlement with dark humor. the supporting cast, including brendan fraser as a savvy investor and max minkowski as eduardo’s lawyer, round out the film with strong performances.\n\ndavid fincher’s direction is sleek and stylish, with a visual palette that reflects the cold, digital world of the early 2000s. the cinematography by trent reznor and atticus ross (who composed the haunting score) enhances the film’s themes of isolation and connection in the digital age. the editing by kirk baxter and angus wall keeps the pacing tight, ensuring the film remains engaging despite its complex narrative structure.\n\nthe social network is more than just a movie about a tech startup; it’s a meditation on friendship, betrayal, and the price of ambition. it questions whether success is worth the personal cost and whether the connections we make online are as meaningful as those we forge in person. the film’s ending, with zuckerberg sending a friend request to his former girlfriend, underscores his loneliness and his inability to connect meaningfully, even as he sits atop a global empire.\n\nin terms of awards and recognition, the film was a critical darling, earning eight academy award nominations, including best picture, best director, and best actor for eisenberg, who took home the golden globe for his performance. it also won three oscars for best film editing, best original score, and best adapted screenplay.\n\nin conclusion, the social network is a brilliant, fast-paced, and thought-provoking film that blends drama, comedy, and tragedy seamlessly. it’s a must-watch for anyone interested in the origins of one of the most influential companies of the 21st century, as well as the human stories behind it. with its sharp script, outstanding performances, and fincher’s signature style, it’s a modern classic that stands the test of time.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.3156192898750305}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.00300444383174181}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -2.0391